# macOS Keychain Provider

The `macoskeychain` provider reads passwords from the macOS Keychain as
[ephemeral values](https://opentofu.org/docs/language/ephemerality/): OpenTofu
and Terraform hand them to the configuration that needs them, such as a provider
block or a write-only argument, and never write them to a plan or state file.

It offers only ephemeral resources. It has no managed resources and no data
sources, because both store their values in the state.

It needs OpenTofu 1.11 or later, or Terraform 1.10 or later, running on macOS.
On any other platform the provider still installs, so `init` and `validate`
work in mixed teams, but every ephemeral resource fails with an error that
explains why.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

Store a secret once from a terminal. With `-w` at the end, `security` prompts
for the value, so it never lands in your shell history:

```shell
security add-generic-password -s sentry-auth-token -a deploy -w
```

Then read it where it is needed:

```terraform
ephemeral "macoskeychain_password" "sentry" {
  service = "sentry-auth-token"
  account = "deploy"
}

provider "sentry" {
  token = ephemeral.macoskeychain_password.sentry.password
}
```

## Security model

- **Nothing reaches the plan or the state.** Every value the provider returns
  is ephemeral, and the `password` attribute is also marked sensitive. OpenTofu
  rejects an ephemeral value in any argument that would be stored.
- **The secret stays in process.** The provider calls the macOS Security
  framework (`SecItemCopyMatching`) directly. It starts no subprocess, so the
  secret never appears on a command line or in an environment, and it never
  logs it. Error messages name only the service or server, the account and the
  keychain path.
- **Read only.** The provider never creates, changes or deletes keychain items.
- **macOS decides who may read an item.** Each item has an access control list
  of trusted applications. The provider does not bypass it; see below.

The provider does not protect a secret from other code running as your user
that the item's access control trusts, and the secret is in the memory of
OpenTofu and of every provider that receives it.

## Granting access to an item

An item created with `security add-generic-password` and no `-T` or `-A`
option trusts only `/usr/bin/security`. The first time the provider reads it,
macOS asks whether to allow access:

- **Allow** grants access for this read only. OpenTofu opens ephemeral
  resources during both plan and apply, so expect to be asked for each.
- **Always Allow** adds this provider binary to the item's access control list.

Release builds are not signed with an Apple Developer ID, so macOS identifies
each provider version by its code hash and asks again after an upgrade.

When no dialog can be shown (an SSH session, CI), the read fails instead. In
that case, unlock the keychain first with `security unlock-keychain`, and
create the item so that the provider may read it without confirmation:

- `security add-generic-password -T /path/to/terraform-provider-macoskeychain_vX.Y.Z ...`
  trusts that one binary, which is the narrower choice.
- `security add-generic-password -A ...` lets any application read the item
  without confirmation. Use it only for keychains dedicated to automation.

